import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.3c — Random Formula: Test, Trace &amp; Connect</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You've run experiments and built a dice roller. Now prove you own the formula
        — not by memorizing it, but by being able to rebuild it from first principles.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 1: Blank Page Challenge</h3>

    <p>Close all tabs. On a blank piece of paper (or a blank file), write:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>The signature and return range of <code>Math.random()</code> — from memory</li>
        <li>The formula for a random integer from <code>min</code> to <code>max</code>, inclusive</li>
        <li>Test it: roll a d20 (1–20) and a d100 (1–100). Verify the range.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>If you had to explain the formula to someone who's never coded, could you? Try it. The real test of understanding is whether you can teach it.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: The Casting Trap — Can You Spot It?</h3>

    <p>For each line below, predict the output <em>before</em> running it:</p>

    <pre><code>System.out.println(  (int) Math.random() * 10  );
System.out.println(  (int) (Math.random() * 10)  );
System.out.println(  (int) (Math.random() * 6) + 1  );
System.out.println(  (int) Math.random() * 6 + 1  );</code></pre>

    <p>Write your predictions. Then test. How many did you get right?</p>

    <h3>Part 3: Connect to the RPG</h3>

    <p>
        In your RPG, a goblin's attack damage is 1d6+2. A dragon's breath is 6d8.
        A critical hit doubles the dice. A stat roll is 4d6-drop-lowest.
    </p>

    <p><strong>For each one:</strong> What's the range? What's the average? Write the formula
    you'd use in Java. (The 4d6-drop-lowest is tricky — you'll need to write it, not just
    think it.)</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Loot Table</h3>
        <p>Design a loot drop system with weighted randomness:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Roll a d100 (1–100)</li>
            <li>1–50: "Common: Iron Sword"</li>
            <li>51–80: "Uncommon: Steel Shield"</li>
            <li>81–95: "Rare: Flaming Axe"</li>
            <li>96–100: "Legendary: Dragon Slayer"</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Run it 20 times. Does the distribution match your design?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write the random formula from scratch, correctly, every time</li>
            <li>Predict what ANY casting/parentheses combination will output</li>
            <li>Design a weighted random system using if-else ranges</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>(int) Math.random() * 6</code> always returns 0 — to a classmate, in plain English</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/math-random.html">Math.random()</a> — full reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/printing.html">Printing &amp; Concatenation</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/three-step-pattern.html">Three-Step Pattern</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
